{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "globalDependencies": ["eslint.config.js", ".prettierrc*", "tsconfig.base.json"],
  // Bound how many package tasks run at once. turbo's default of 10 multiplies
  // with each test task's own Vitest worker pool. Paired with `maxWorkers` in
  // vitest.config.ts, which explains the measurements and the tradeoff: this cuts
  // process count and oversubscription, not peak CPU. A percentage keeps it
  // proportional on a small CI runner. Measured: 25% cost 30% more wall time for
  // no further benefit, so 50% is the chosen point.
  "concurrency": "50%",
  // turbo 2.x runs tasks in **strict** env mode by default: a task sees only the
  // variables declared here plus turbo's own defaults. Every Testcontainers and
  // Docker knob this repo documents was therefore invisible to `turbo run test`,
  // silently. That is not theoretical - it is how `postgres:16-alpine` kept being
  // pulled from Docker Hub inside the `verify` job long after issue #74 pointed
  // QCMS_TEST_POSTGRES_IMAGE at a GHCR mirror: the variable reached the job, not
  // the Vitest process, so the harness asked for the default reference, which was
  // not the pre-pulled one, and Docker went to Hub for it. The `api-e2e` and
  // `portal-e2e` jobs never had the bug because they invoke Vitest and Playwright
  // directly, without turbo (issue #150).
  //
  // passThrough rather than `env`: these choose *where* an identical image comes
  // from and whether a cleanup sidecar runs, so they must not be part of the task
  // hash - hashing them would miss the cache on every machine that resolves a
  // different mirror, for byte-identical results.
  //
  // Verify a new knob actually arrives with an unpullable value: if
  // `QCMS_TEST_POSTGRES_IMAGE=localhost:1/nope pnpm exec turbo run test
  // --filter @qcms/db --force` PASSES, the variable is being stripped.
  // The `OTEL_*` knobs (task 054 / ADR-34) are deliberately NOT listed below, and
  // that is the safe direction of the strict-env trap rather than an oversight. No
  // turbo-run task needs them: the unit and integration suites never start an SDK
  // (they assert the gate instead), and the traced proof lives in the Playwright
  // suite, which runs without turbo and sets `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` itself
  // (pointed at its own in-test receiver, `apps/portal/e2e/support/otlp-receiver.ts`).
  // Passing them through would mean a developer who exports an endpoint for the
  // local trace viewer silently turns exporting on inside `pnpm test`. If a future
  // turbo-run task ever must read one, add it here and prove it arrives the way the
  // Postgres-image note below describes.
  //
  // `QCMS_PORT_SEAT` (issue #255) is listed for exactly the reason above. It picks
  // both the `7Sxx` and `17Sxx` blocks this machine seat binds (docs/PORTS.md), and
  // the portal project's unit tests read it through
  // `apps/portal/e2e/support/harness-config.ts`. Without the passthrough a seat
  // running `QCMS_PORT_SEAT=1 pnpm test` would see seat 0 inside Vitest and seat 1
  // everywhere else, which is the same silent-divergence class the whole issue is
  // about. It is passThrough rather than `env` because the seat chooses *where*
  // identical work binds, not what the work computes: hashing it would miss the cache
  // on every seat for byte-identical results.
  "globalPassThroughEnv": [
    "QCMS_PORT_SEAT",
    "QCMS_TEST_POSTGRES_IMAGE",
    "TESTCONTAINERS_RYUK_DISABLED",
    "RYUK_CONTAINER_IMAGE",
    "TESTCONTAINERS_HOST_OVERRIDE",
    "DOCKER_HOST",
    "DOCKER_CONFIG",
    "DOCKER_AUTH_CONFIG"
  ],
  "tasks": {
    // The probe task behind `scripts/turbo-passthrough-env.test.ts` (issue #154). It
    // prints what actually reached a task turbo ran, which is the only way to observe
    // strict env mode from outside: a test that reads `process.env` inside Vitest is
    // running on the wrong side of the boundary that strips the variable.
    //
    // A root task (`//#`) so no workspace package carries a script it does not own, and
    // `"cache": false` because the whole value of the run is that it EXECUTED - a
    // replayed log would report a previous run's environment, which is the same
    // false-green shape the test exists to catch.
    "//#env-arrival": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**"]
    },
    // An `outputs` glob must match only files the build itself writes: turbo tars
    // whatever matches when the task ends, and a later cache hit restores that over
    // whatever is on disk, in any worktree (issue #57).
    //
    // `next build` writes `apps/portal/.next`. Two subdirectories under it are
    // carved back out:
    //   - `dev` is a dev server's live working directory. Since issue #54 the
    //     portal's dev phase writes `.next-dev` instead (see next.config.ts), so
    //     nothing writes `.next/dev` here any more, but the glob still named it, and
    //     residue left by an older checkout or a stray `next dev` was being tarred
    //     into the shared artifact and restored elsewhere: measured at 277 MB of dev
    //     Turbopack cache and a dev log, against a 16 MB build. A planted marker file
    //     under `.next/dev` was overwritten by a cache hit before this exclusion and
    //     survives one after it.
    //   - `cache` is Next's incremental cache between builds (a `.tsbuildinfo` from
    //     the build's typecheck), not build product, and it is where a running
    //     server would keep any data or image cache. Excluding it keeps a cache hit
    //     from putting an older copy over a newer one.
    "qcms-portal#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": [".next/**", "!.next/cache/**", "!.next/dev/**"]
    },
    // The admin (task 031) writes `apps/admin/.next` and needs the same entry for the
    // same reasons, plus one of its own: without it the generic `build` task's
    // `dist/**` glob applies, matches nothing the admin produces, and turbo warns
    // "no output files found" on every run while caching none of the build. The
    // carve-outs mirror the portal's - `dev` because a stray dev directory under
    // `.next` would be tarred into the shared artifact and restored over live dev
    // state in any worktree (issue #54/#57), `cache` because Next's incremental cache
    // is not build product and a hit must not put an older copy over a newer one.
    "qcms-admin#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": [".next/**", "!.next/cache/**", "!.next/dev/**"]
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["build"]
    },
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"]
    },
    // Lint depends on build for the same reason typecheck does, and it was the one
    // task that never said so (issue #560). ESLint runs with typed rules here
    // (`parserOptions.projectService` in `eslint.config.js`), so
    // `@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-*` reads the same declaration files `tsc` does.
    // With no `dependsOn` at all, `turbo run lint --filter=<pkg>` in a tree whose
    // `dist/` directories are not present resolved every workspace import to `error`
    // and reported unsafe-assignment violations that say nothing about the code. It
    // only ever passed because `pnpm verify` runs `pnpm build` first and because a
    // shared turbo cache replayed a green from a machine that had built.
    "lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"]
    },
    // The admin's two program-shaped tasks need TWO packages the admin itself does
    // not depend on, so `^build` cannot reach them (issue #560, root cause of #632
    // and of item 1 in #387).
    //
    // `apps/admin/tsconfig.json` includes `**/*.ts`, which takes in the Playwright
    // harness under `apps/admin/e2e/`. Two of those files import apps/api SOURCE by
    // relative path (`e2e/support/ops.ts` and `e2e/support/admin-account.ts` reach
    // `../../../api/e2e/support/*` and `../../../api/src/*`), so the admin's tsc
    // program transitively contains the API's source tree, which imports `@qcms/db`
    // and `@qcms/a2ui-compiler`. Both resolve through `apps/api/node_modules`, and
    // both publish their types from `dist`, so an unbuilt tree gives 30-odd
    // "Cannot find module '@qcms/db'" errors and the implicit-`any` cascade behind
    // them - in a package whose own manifest names neither.
    //
    // Declared here rather than by adding the two to `apps/admin/package.json`: the
    // admin imports neither, and task 056 deliberately left it with no database
    // client of its own (see the note in `apps/admin/e2e/support/admin-account.ts`),
    // which a manifest dependency would quietly undo. This is a build-order fact
    // about the admin's typecheck program, and this is where build order lives.
    "qcms-admin#typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "@qcms/db#build", "@qcms/a2ui-compiler#build"]
    },
    "qcms-admin#lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "@qcms/db#build", "@qcms/a2ui-compiler#build"]
    },
    // The portal's two program-shaped tasks have exactly the same undeclared reach as
    // the admin's, and never saying so is issue #765.
    //
    // `apps/portal/tsconfig.json` includes `**/*.ts`, which takes in the Playwright
    // harness under `apps/portal/e2e/`. `e2e/support/api-server.ts` and
    // `e2e/support/api-process-entry.ts` import apps/api SOURCE by relative path, so
    // the portal's tsc program transitively contains the API's source tree, which
    // imports `@qcms/db`, `@qcms/csv` and `@qcms/a2ui-compiler`. All three publish
    // their types from `dist`, and the portal's own manifest names none of them.
    //
    // Undeclared, that is a latent build-order bug of the #560 shape. What turned it
    // into a FLAKE is that since issue #494 every emitting package's build removes its
    // own `dist` before `tsc` runs. With no edge, `qcms-portal#typecheck` starts as
    // soon as `@qcms/ui` and `@qcms/observability` are built, while `@qcms/db#build` -
    // pulled into the same run by some OTHER filter - is sitting between its
    // `clean-dist` and its `tsc`, so the portal's tsc reads a `dist` that is not there.
    // That is why `pnpm exec turbo run typecheck --filter=qcms-admin
    // --filter=qcms-portal --force` failed with 40-odd TS2307s while either filter
    // ALONE was green: alone, nothing asks for `@qcms/db#build`, the previous build's
    // `dist` is never removed, and the undeclared read quietly succeeds. The clean is
    // not the defect - it is what made a missing edge observable.
    //
    // Declared here rather than by adding the three to `apps/portal/package.json`: the
    // portal holds no database credential and reaches the API over the internal
    // network (R2, ADR-35), and a manifest dependency on `@qcms/db` would quietly undo
    // that. This is a build-order fact about the portal's typecheck program, and this
    // is where build order lives. `apps/e2e` takes the other route for the same
    // problem, with its reason written in `apps/e2e/tsconfig.json`.
    "qcms-portal#typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "@qcms/db#build", "@qcms/a2ui-compiler#build", "@qcms/csv#build"]
    },
    "qcms-portal#lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "@qcms/db#build", "@qcms/a2ui-compiler#build", "@qcms/csv#build"]
    },
    // `apps/e2e` declares `@qcms/db` as a devDependency (the reason is in its
    // tsconfig), which reaches `@qcms/core` and `@qcms/a2ui-compiler` through that
    // package's own `^build`. It reaches neither `@qcms/csv` nor `@qcms/observability`,
    // and its one spec consumes both through `../admin/e2e/support/*` and the API
    // source behind it. Two missing edges of the portal's class, found by the same
    // derived check rather than by a third failing run.
    "qcms-e2e#typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "@qcms/csv#build", "@qcms/observability#build"]
    },
    "qcms-e2e#lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "@qcms/csv#build", "@qcms/observability#build"]
    }
  }
}
